A||||1||gbv||a few O2 sensor questions||||5||
Z||000000||gbv||04-18-2000||12:13 PM||||i used the search function but couldnt find what im looking for.<P>i replaced the O2 sensor today. now, to remove the old one, which obviously hadnt been changed in a LONG time, i used some liquid wrench. now, when i start the car, a few seconds after theres a light smoke coming from the sensor location. is this just the leftover liquid wrench burning away, or does the O2 sensor have a "burn-in" period? or maybe it didnt tighten it enough? (i tightened it the most i could)<P>also, do i need to reset the computer when i change the O2 sensor? and something along the lines of driving over a certain speed for 30mins?<P>any help appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||04-18-2000||12:29 PM||||Well yeah, you're burning off the lubricant. Watch it untill it stops smoking just to make sure it doesn't light up. (Liquid Wrench is more dificult to lite than WD40 and CRC 556 but may under some conditions.)<P>no burn in. tho any surface residue from manufacturing may burn off with the lubricant you sprayed all over the old one.<P>You don't have to reset the ECM. It doesn't hurt but you don't have to. The ECM will rework the "learned" data table over the next few days. Reseting just makes the ECM start from scratch. It will still need a few days to settle the learned table either way. (It never completely stops adjusting the learn table.)<P>The driving thing it to make the ECM home the IAC valve. You have to get the engine over so many RPM for more than 30 sec so the ECM has time to close the IAC without stalling the car. This actually happens every time the car is run. It's just that after an ECM reset you will likely get a squirly idle untill the first time the ECM does an IAC reset.||||reg||1
